{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004158","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 22 candidates are direct matches at the EFO level (chronic kidney disease), but several have endpoint/covariate issues for a generic CKD target. Per the provided domain policy for CKD, stage-specific and heavily covariate-adjusted CKD models (e.g., PGS002237 with CKD stage 3+ and extensive clinical covariates including hypertension/diabetes/BMI/medications) should not outrank a coherent generic CKD family with no added clinical covariates; similarly, framework/pan-trait or proxy endpoints are down-ranked (e.g., PGS001272 snpnet UKB pan-trait TTE chronic renal failure; PGS000728 uses eGFR; PGS000859 has a diabetes-subtype phenotype; PGS004224 is exposure-specific; PGS004889 is incident CKD without diabetes and includes a clinical risk score).

Among the cleanest endpoint-faithful options, the 2023 AJHG ‘five biobanks’ cluster (PGS003988, PGS004004, PGS004016, PGS004030, PGS004045, PGS004058, PGS004074, PGS004088, PGS004101, PGS004112, PGS004128, PGS004142, PGS004158) consistently uses a direct CKD phenotype (‘chronic kidney disease or dialysis’) with covariates listed as 0, avoiding clinical risk-factor packaging. Within that coherent family, PGS004158 (UKBB-EUR.MultiPRS.CV) has the strongest reported full-model discrimination and variance explained in the selected European validation record (AUROC 0.5917; R² 0.0200) and similar/stronger effect-size evidence (OR 1.389) relative to its siblings, so it is the best-supported choice from the visible evidence.

The main limitation is that most candidates (including PGS004158) provide only full-model AUROC/R² without explicit PRS-only metrics, and the phenotype includes ‘or dialysis’ (a composite), so comparability and endpoint specificity are not perfect; additionally, an apparently very strong AFR-only model (PGS005090, AUROC 0.9) cannot be fairly prioritized for a generic/unspecified ancestry use case from this context alone and may reflect non-comparable reporting."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait “sleep apnea,” including multiple “Sleep apnea” models (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several “Obstructive sleep apnea” models (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Per the provided domain_knowledge, obstructive sleep apnea is the clinically dominant subtype of sleep apnea and should be treated as a strong direct match when better supported.

Among the OSA-labeled candidates, PGS005220 is best supported in the visible evidence because it comes from a disease-focused publication (“Polygenic scores for obstructive sleep apnoea…”) with multi-cohort development (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP), reports the strongest validation discrimination (full_model AUROC=0.79; record_count=1) with a sizable validation sample (n=21,975), and shows a clear association strength (OR=1.53, 95% CI 1.39–1.68). Compared with other direct matches, the ExPRSweb “Sleep apnea” scores have near-chance full-model AUROC (~0.49–0.527), and PGS003479 has small validation (n=1,115) and unclear/possibly inconsistent phenotype metadata (“phenotyping_reported: DBP”).

However, the choice remains sub-optimal rather than high-quality because the reported AUROC is a full-model metric (not explicitly PRS-only) and the covariates include BMI (a major mediator/risk factor for OSA), making the discrimination less comparable as standalone PRS evidence; also, the alternative BMI-adjusted OSA score (PGS005219) has the same AUROC (0.79) but a larger OR (1.98), leaving unresolved ambiguity about which endpoint packaging is preferable for a generic “sleep apnea” target given only the provided metadata."}
